  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper level ridge axis (~591 DAM at 500 MB) slides east through \nthe central Great Lakes this evening. Height falls tonight/tomorrow \nas a shortwave trough tracking through Montana this afternoon moves \nthrough Lake Superior. A moisture axis (PW values around 2 inches) \nover the Midwest to fold over/northeast into southern Lower Michigan \nWednesday morning, with a cold front slowly tracking through \nsoutheast Michigan during the day. Showers are likely, but clouds \nand earlier timing likely limit instability, supporting only a \nchance of thunderstorms. The far southeast section of the CWA (ADG-\nDTW-PHN) stands the best chance of building up enough instability to \ndraw some concern for strong thunderstorms, as wind fields are \nelevated (40 knots 850 MB). As such, gradient winds during the day \nwill increase ahead of the cold front, and local probabilistic \nguidance suggesting wind gusts 25-30 mph.  Machine learning also \nindicating a 2-5% percent chance of damaging winds over southern \nMonroe county, in line with the SPC Day 2 outlook. Any marginally \nsevere chance will be dependent on later timing of activity. \n\nJust a modest cooldown is expected for Thursday as 850 MB temps dip \nback toward 10 C underneath drying northwest winds. Fast pseudo-\nzonal flow over northern CONUS with an upper level ridge arriving \nFriday should maintain dry weather into Saturday morning before a \nlow chance of showers arrives for the second half of the weekend. \nThe amplitude of the trough swinging through varies amongst the \nmedium range models. None-the-less, euro ensembles support just a \nmodest cool down for Sunday, with temperatures remaining slightly \nabove normal.",
